Here is the complete, brutal Red Team & Reverse Engineering Audit Checklist in English, structured so you can test every single layer of your Safe Rust protector before considering it finished.

Red Team Attack & Validation Checklist for a Polymorphic Code Virtualizer

To survive real-world adversarial reverse engineering (at the level of analysts who break commercial protectors like VMProtect, Denuvo, or analyze nation-state implants), your protector must pass 10 brutal attack vectors:

1. Static Lifting & Automated Decompilation

• The Attack: Load the protected binary into Ghidra, IDA Pro (Hex-Rays), and Binary Ninja and run their automated decompilers on the protected sections.
• What the Red Team Looks For:
	• Whether the decompiler successfully identifies the main Virtual Machine dispatcher loop (switch table) and reconstructs readable C-like logic.
	• Leftover Rust compiler artifacts in .rdata or .rodata—such as panic strings (index out of bounds), source file paths, or core::panicking symbols—that immediately pinpoint the VM engine.
	• Hardcoded magic constants (cryptographic S-boxes, license check values) sitting in plaintext inside the binary.
• Pass Criteria: Decompilers must either choke on control-flow complexity/function size or output thousands of lines of tangled, non-reducible state transitions with zero recognizable Rust strings or plaintext constants.

2. Automated MBA Simplification (AST & Program Synthesis Attacks)

• The Attack: Analysts feed your obfuscated arithmetic blocks into specialized de-obfuscation tools like GAMBA, SiMBA, MBA-Blast, or Triton to reduce complex expressions back into simple operations (such as x + y or x == y).
• What the Red Team Looks For: Shallow Mixed Boolean-Arithmetic (MBA) rules or textbook identities that automated truth-table or algebraic solvers collapse in milliseconds.
• Pass Criteria: You must combine linear and non-linear MBA transformations (mixing bitwise operations with modular multiplication and variable shifts) nested multiple layers deep, causing automated simplifiers to time out or fail reduction.

3. Dynamic Binary Instrumentation (DBI) & Instruction Tracing

• The Attack: Run the protected binary under Intel PIN, Frida, DynamoRIO, or QEMU to record every single native CPU instruction executed from start to finish (an execution trace), then apply automated Dead Code Elimination.
• What the Red Team Looks For:
	• Static fake branches (Opaque Predicates) become completely useless during a dynamic trace because the CPU only executes the true path—automatically stripping away 90% of your decoy code.
	• Using frequency analysis on the recorded trace, the attacker identifies the most frequently executed instruction blocks to map out core VM handlers (VM_ADD, VM_XOR, VM_LOAD).
• Pass Criteria:
	• VM handlers must be structurally uniform, share overlapping code blocks, and mutate internal state on every step so the same handler performs different semantic actions depending on execution context.
	• The runtime must detect DBI instrumentation overhead (via subtle cache-timing anomalies and cycle-count drifts caused by JIT recompilation engines like PIN and Frida) and silently corrupt execution.

4. Dynamic Taint Analysis

• The Attack: Using tools like Triton or Panda, the attacker marks user-controlled input bytes (such as a wrong license key or password) as "tainted" and tracks every CPU register and memory address touched by those bytes.
• What the Red Team Looks For: They ignore 99% of your VM overhead and look strictly at the instructions that process tainted data, quickly isolating the exact comparison routine.
• Pass Criteria: Taint Explosion or Taint Laundering. Input bytes must be mathematically mixed into the VM's internal state (such as the rolling bytecode decryption key) or routed through non-linear lookup tables (S-boxes) so taint trackers either lose the data flow entirely or mark the entire process memory as tainted.

5. Symbolic Execution & SMT Solvers

• The Attack: Load the virtualized routine into angr or KLEE, define the target condition ("find any input that leads to the success state"), and let an SMT solver (like Z3) mathematically solve for the valid input without understanding your code.
• What the Red Team Looks For: Pure arithmetic or linear bitwise logic without one-way cryptographic operations, allowing Z3 to reverse the math automatically.
• Pass Criteria: Embed cryptographic mixing rounds (such as SipHash or ChaCha quarter-rounds) directly into your Control Flow Flattening state transitions and use input-dependent loop bounds, forcing symbolic execution engines into exponential path explosion.

6. Live Memory Dumping & Unpacking

• The Attack: Let the executable launch, initialize itself in RAM, and perform a full process memory dump using x64dbg, Scylla, or Process Hacker to inspect the unpacked memory image.
• What the Red Team Looks For: Whether the virtual bytecode sits decrypted in heap or section memory once execution begins. If it does, they dump it and write a custom disassembler for your VM.
• Pass Criteria: The bytecode must never exist decrypted in RAM. Each instruction byte is decrypted inside a CPU register at the exact moment it is fetched, consumed immediately, and overwritten.

7. Byte Patching & Forced Execution

• The Attack: Instead of reverse-engineering your VM, the attacker locates the caller function in a debugger and simply patches the return value to true (or overwrites the VM call with NOP instructions).
• What the Red Team Looks For: Whether your protection is isolated inside a standalone check function that merely returns a boolean (0 or 1). If it is, no amount of obfuscation matters.
• Pass Criteria: Deep Code Binding. The VM must never simply return a yes/no verdict. Instead, it must compute essential runtime values required by the rest of the application to function—such as decrypting critical configuration structures, deriving function pointers, or generating keys needed to unpack subsequent assets. Bypassing the VM must leave the host program starved of valid data so it crashes naturally later.

8. Hypervisor & Kernel-Level Debugging

• The Attack: Execute your protected binary inside a stealthy hypervisor debugger (such as HyperDbg or a custom KVM build) that hides debugger flags, spoofs CPU timestamp counters (RDTSC), and uses hardware breakpoints (DR0–DR3) that do not modify code bytes in memory (bypassing CRC self-checks).
• What the Red Team Looks For: Reliance on standard user-mode anti-debug APIs or simple software breakpoint (0xCC) checksums.
• Pass Criteria: Combine thread-context inspection (checking debug registers via exception-handling context frames) with dependent latency chains and external timing correlations that cannot be trivially spoofed by hypervisor instruction interception.

9. Differential Binary Analysis (BinDiff / Diaphora)

• The Attack: Protect the exact same test binary twice (generating Build A and Build B) and run BinDiff or Diaphora to compare the two outputs side by side.
• What the Red Team Looks For: Anything that did not change between builds. Static dispatcher skeletons, identical handler layouts, or shared constants immediately expose the structural blueprint of your VM.
• Pass Criteria: Polymorphism must mutate everything across builds: opcode byte assignments, handler memory layout, virtual register count and ordering, operand encoding formats, and dispatcher control flow.

10. Adversarial Fuzzing of the VM Runtime

• The Attack: Aim a coverage-guided fuzzer (AFL++ or libFuzzer) directly at your VM runtime, feeding it thousands of mutated, truncated, and corrupted bytecode streams.
• What the Red Team Looks For: Whether modifying a single byte in the protected section causes an out-of-bounds read, an infinite loop, or an exploitable memory corruption bug inside your VM interpreter.
• Pass Criteria: Zero panics (panic handlers stripped/aborted in release builds), deterministic termination via an instruction step counter ("gas limit") to prevent infinite loops, and 100% memory-safe handling of corrupted bytecode guaranteed by Safe Rust.
